\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Simulated outcomes.} All outcomes come from six mechanisms of our choosing, four smooth in exposure, with Gaussian noise. \emph{Graph range.} The $d$ exponent rests on three degrees; thinning real graphs also changes clustering and the degree tail. \emph{Estimators.} Causal message passing and graph neural networks were not run (Section~\ref{sec:design}); the adaptive H\'ajek estimator is simplified; flexible learners use 200 draws per graph and fixed tunings. \emph{Design.} Mechanism (e) at $s=0.9$ is truncated at $K=30$ steps (its population-OLS prediction is outside the Monte Carlo interval in 10\% of its cells).

\paragraph{Implications.}
Regression adjustment pays when spillover is large and the response smooth; report the leverage $L$ and the estimated spillover share with the estimate. Agreement with richer specifications is not evidence of small bias, and the default interval cannot be trusted when the response may be curved or thresholded. Whether a bias bound from data in the observed exposure range exists is open.
